# Cloudflare Pages custom response headers.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# GitHub Pages sends `cache-control: max-age=600` on everything with no way to
# override it. For an installed iOS PWA that is the confirmed cause of repeated
# staleness: Safari holds the HTML shell AND sw.js for up to 10 minutes, so a
# deploy is invisible until both expire. Pages lets us set the headers directly.
#
# This file is the network-cache half of the caching design. The other half is
# sw.js (the service worker cache). They are deliberately complementary:
#
#   asset class          _headers (browser/CDN)   sw.js (service worker)
#   -------------------  -----------------------  ----------------------
#   sw.js                no-store                 network-only (passthrough)
#   *.html               no-store                 network (never SW-cached)
#   version.json         no-store                 network (never SW-cached)
#   ?v= JS / CSS         immutable, 1 year        cache-first
#   icons / fonts        immutable, 1 year        cache-first
#
# Rule count: 10 of the 100-rule limit (verified against the documented cap).

# --- Always-fresh: the three files that control everything else -------------
# sw.js (the update entry point — if it is cached, no fix can ever ship) and
# version.json (polled by pwa.js on load and every 10 minutes; a cached copy
# would defeat the self-heal reload) are both covered by the `/*` rule below,
# which is no-store. They are called out here only as documentation; adding
# explicit rules would duplicate the value, since matching rules combine.

# HTML shells carry the ?v= stamps that retire old JS/CSS. Stale HTML pins
# stale asset URLs, so the shell must always be fetched fresh.
#
# NOTE: Pages 308-redirects /portal.html to the extensionless /portal, so the
# canonical URL a user actually lands on matches NEITHER `/` nor `/*.html`.
# Verified by curl: with only those two rules, /portal, /client, /dashboard and
# every other page came back with NO Cache-Control header at all. So the guard
# is a catch-all `/*` (no-store by default), and each genuinely cacheable path
# below re-opts into caching. Because overlapping rules COMBINE their headers,
# those rules must first `! Cache-Control` to drop the inherited no-store —
# otherwise they would emit "no-store, ..., immutable" and cache nothing.
/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

# --- Immutable: URLs that change identity when their content changes --------
# JS/CSS are requested with ?v=<stamp>, so a new build is a new URL. The URL
# itself is therefore immutable and safe to cache hard.
#
# These are listed individually rather than as `/*.js` on purpose. Overlapping
# rules INHERIT each other's headers and duplicate header names are joined with
# a comma, so a `/*.js` rule would also match /sw.js and emit the contradictory
# `Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate, public, max-age=31536000`
# — silently un-fixing the exact bug this file exists to fix.
/nav.js
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/gm.js
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/pwa.js
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/datetime.js
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/analytics-view.js
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*.css
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/icons/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/manifest.json
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
